  "subtitle": "A one-handed NES controller steered by gyroscopic tilt, sold as something that 'changes the software you already own'",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum's earliest commercial gyroscopic game controller — a one-hand tilt-steered flight-stick-style pad from IMN Control (1992) that predates the Nintendo Wii's motion-sensing by a full decade and the Playstation DualShock gyro by far more. Its marketing claimed the tilt sensing let it 'change pre-existing software you already own,' producing moves a standard pad cannot express — like running Mario backwards. That claim is the interaction model: an attitude-sensing controller that reads the controller's own orientation in space as input, years before gyroscopes became normal. It fills a genuine gap in the collection, which has body-grammar grips (Fairchild Channel F), touch surfaces (Turbo Touch 360), analog rollers (Coleco Super Action) and force/pneumatic pads, but no gyroscopic/attitude-sensing input. DEFERRED for images: no freely-licensed photo exists on Commons (verified); the only surviving visuals are a rights-managed 1992 EGM magazine advertisement and stills from the bundled instructional VHS.",
